grounds:[{location:"/definitions/2",reason:"The spaces are completions under nonnegative weighted Fourier square sums."},
{location:"/definitions/1",reason:"Zero mean is vanishing of frequency zero and reality is conjugate symmetry."},
{location:p.s2,reason:"The uses have epsilon<=1/8 and a_sigma<=1+sigma."},
{location:p.s3,reason:"The path construction uses Fourier truncations of constant mean-zero H^sigma data."}],
justification:"Since <k>>=1, <k>^(2*u)<=<k>^(2*v), giving the norm inequality on finite sums and a continuous extension by completion. The squared truncation error is a tail of the convergent nonnegative defining series, hence tends to zero. Deleting modes decreases its sum; a symmetric truncation preserves opposite-frequency conjugacy and keeps frequency zero zero. These facts validate the embeddings and input approximations, but do not themselves supply the missing base-zero source bound."
